{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-16T06:23:05.963Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"FYI from michi:\n\n\n\n<pasted_content id=\"5929\">\nI'm reading \"michi\" in your list as Monokl and Wisp, since michi is us. Each message below rests on what I verified earlier, and I haven't checked Caliper's or Monokl's code against 0.10.\n\nTo all consumers: what we are and aren't\n- Lead with the response contract, not TOON. michi renders what a caller has already decided into agent-readable text, and gives one shape for the things every tool re-invents: sections, a budget report, provenance, per-op outcomes, truncation signals, hints, recovery steps, redaction and retry math.\n- Don't oversell TOON. Measured: the same accuracy as JSON and Markdown, about 47% fewer tokens than pretty JSON and only about 8% fewer than a Markdown table.\n- Availability: 0.9.0 is published today. The envelope ships in 0.10.0, which is not out yet (#116). It is pre-1.0, and a minor bump can break. The whole family moves in lockstep.\n- Licensing: michi-graph is MIT. michi-core and michi-pipeline are FSL-1.1-MIT.\n</pasted_content id=\"5929\">\n\n\nCaliper\n- Sections now express its nested, heterogeneous output (components, props, diagnostics, stats), and the truncation signal can replace its inline ...(+N) marker. Its own escaping differs from michi's TOON 4.4, so we should compare against the same fixtures before it adopts anything. Keep its canonical JSON as the source of truth."}
