{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-16T17:25:43.953Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a40ef4ba71b5e804e</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_016WouY9EE9ohAXrvJ1gXUAS</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper/ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba/tasks/a40ef4ba71b5e804e.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium de016f69..89275198\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>The review of `de016f69..89275198` found 10 candidates. As the high-effort recipe asks, I deduplicated them but did not verify them and did not run any tests. The first three are the most likely to be real bugs; 4–6 are smaller correctness edge cases; 7–10 are cleanup.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/pipeline/revision.rs\",\n    \"line\": 154,\n    \"summary\": \"`layers_differ` still compares only `inheritance`, on the old assumption that relevance writes only `used` lists. Relevance now also removes rows from `props`/`notable_inherited` through `narrow`, so a change to the listing alone is not reported.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A component keeps its resolution. An edit at a call site pushes a member over or under the call-site floor. If another layer already holds that name, `narrow` skips the layer push (mod.rs:117), so the inheritance is unchanged while `props` gains or loses the row. `redecided()` and the eager check at line 666 return false, so watch mode never sends the updated component. Comparing the whole answer (props + notable_inherited + inheritance) would fix it.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 151,\n    \"summary\": \"An `Omit` whose keys are not a literal union (most often `keyof OwnProps`) sets `declined`. That turns off all narrowing for the component, even though omitted keys can only remove members.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`interface Props extends Omit&lt;ComponentProps&lt;'div'&gt;, keyof OwnProps&gt;` is a very common pattern. `keys()` reaches `_ =&gt; self.declined = true`, `apply` sets `left` to empty, and every DOM attribute the collapse rule lists comes back as a row (the 280-row badge case this change set out to fix). The `Omit` arm should not decline on its keys.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 170,\n    \"summary\": \"The same `Omit` is declined or not depending on how it is spelled. Written inline it is declined; behind an alias, its keys are never checked.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`props: Omit&lt;X, keyof Y&gt;` is declined and narrows nothing. `type P = Omit&lt;X, keyof Y&gt;; props: P` takes the alias branch, which only recurses into `base`, so it narrows. The same type gets two different listings.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/types/semantic.rs\",\n    \"line\": 434,\n    \"summary\": \"The whole-project merge used to keep `key` when a component with no layers listed it. It now goes through `react_prop_listed`, which always rejects REACT_SPECIAL_PROPS, and `Unanswered::Drop` then removes the structural prop.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A context `Provider` or `th`/`td` intersection whose structural answer lists `key` loses it in the CLI/whole-project lane. The session lane (`Unanswered::Keep`) keeps it, so the lanes disagree again. The old comment said `key` stays when the component lists it.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 167,\n    \"summary\": \"The alias `Pick` branch passes the `picks` recursion depth to `keys()` as its alias depth. Two separate limits get mixed.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A `Pick` reached through MAX_ALIAS_DEPTH-1 alias hops, whose keys are a named literal-union alias, reaches `keys()` with depth == MAX_ALIAS_DEPTH. The `depth &lt; MAX_ALIAS_DEPTH` guard fails, `_ =&gt; declined = true` runs, and narrowing is turned off for a key set that is valid.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/extractor/body_facts.rs\",\n    \"line\": 248,\n    \"summary\": \"`passes_children` treats whitespace differently from the JSX transform. It only counts `\\\\n` as a line break, and Rust's `trim()` also strips Unicode whitespace such as NBSP, which Babel/TS keep.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`&lt;Foo&gt;\\\\r  &lt;/Foo&gt;` (CR-only line endings) is counted as passing `children`, though the transform drops it. `&lt;Foo&gt;\\\\n\\\\u00a0\\\\n&lt;/Foo&gt;` passes an NBSP child but is counted as none. Either way the call-site `children` counts for the floor are wrong.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 108,\n    \"summary\": \"`narrow` allocates three Strings per listed prop just to probe `left`, and uses linear Vec scans (`layer.members.contains`) per removed prop. It runs at least twice per component (apply, then each checker merge).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A Chakra component with about 400 listed props and layers of about 400 members does about 1,200 allocations and about 160k string compares per call, multiplied across every component and lane. This is on the hot path the memory/time budget is measured on. Borrowed lookups (a map keyed by name, then (type, file)) plus a name set built once would avoid it. `sources()` at line 76 has the same pattern.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/react_types.rs\",\n    \"line\": 427,\n    \"summary\": \"`checker_prop_used` and `react_props_wanted` keep an unused `_mode` parameter. `react_props_wanted` always returns `Some`, and `checker_prop_used` is now just a forwarding wrapper for `react_prop_listed`.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Callers still thread `HtmlAttributeMode` through and handle a `None` (meaning \\\"all\\\") case that can no longer happen. Each merge also has two names for one rule. The parameter, the `Option`, and the wrapper should be removed.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 70,\n    \"summary\": \"`read()` now runs `pick()` on the zero-argument props type name, and `picks` follows aliases. The `match lookup(...)` just below then handles the same alias again (Pick → `keys`, Passthrough/Partial → `part` → `pick`).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`type Props = Pick&lt;X, 'a' | 'b'&gt;` resolves its keys twice. Passthrough/Partial/Required aliases are walked twice through `part()`. The work is wasted, and two code paths decide `declined` for the same alias, which is how the inconsistency in the third finding appeared.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 99,\n    \"summary\": \"One line of the `narrow` doc comment is 153 characters, over the repo's `max_width = 120` (rustfmt.toml).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"rustfmt does not wrap doc comments here, so the overlong line stays and breaks the 120-column convention the rest of the file follows.\"\n  }\n]\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>91927</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>13</tool_uses><duration_ms>187294</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
