{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:58:22.593Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a94531663184643a3</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01SbNk8Vsrt33sgN4MFchpqb</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper/ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba/tasks/a94531663184643a3.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium 1335c3d2..46d28434\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 574,\n    \"summary\": \"In a Teardown::OneShot run, `drop(converters.take())` now drops the lanes' converters and their memos synchronously whenever any generic component exists, and `release_lanes().await` waits on release round-trips. OneShot exists to skip exactly this teardown work.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`caliper-docgen extract` on Chakra, which has generic components: the converters are dropped here, so the later `std::mem::forget(converters)` forgets `None`. The 35-50 ms teardown saving from the OneShot changelog comes back on every project with a generic component. Fix: when `request.teardown == OneShot`, `mem::forget` the taken converters instead of dropping them, or decide this on purpose and say so.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 557,\n    \"summary\": \"`layered` now sends the probe file through `updateSnapshot` changes (`fileSystem` together with `reconfigurePrograms`) instead of `createSnapshot`. The docs only establish that the `fileSystem` layer is read on `createSnapshot`, and the new unit test only exercises tsrs.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"If the Go `7.1.0-beta` checker, or a tsrs version, ignores `fileSystem` inside `updateSnapshot` changes, the reconfigured program has a root file that does not exist. `source_file_symbol(probe)` returns None, and every generic component quietly falls back to its structural props (\\\"the checker did not load the file...\\\"). The output regresses with no error.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 571,\n    \"summary\": \"The project id is taken as `response.projects.into_iter().next()`, the first project listed. It is not matched against `self.project`, and nothing checks that the reconfigure actually produced the program rooted at the probe file.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"In the on-demand path, `layered` derives from the kept watch snapshot. If the update response lists another project before the reconfigured one (the comment in `beside` says responses list programs made or changed), the returned Rpc's `project` points at the wrong program. Probe lookups then fail and all generic components fall back, or they read from the wrong program.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 575,\n    \"summary\": \"`release_lanes` permanently releases `programs[1..]`'s snapshots, but those `Arc&lt;Rpc&gt;` handles are still alive in `answer_opened` and still carry their now-dead snapshot ids. Nothing marks them as released.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Any later code that asks a lane after `answer_on`, such as a future retry or a reuse of `programs` by an early start, sends requests against released snapshots and gets Rejected errors. The only protection is the doc comment \\\"Nothing may ask a lane after\\\". Lanes should be consumed or the snapshot ids cleared so misuse is impossible.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 904,\n    \"summary\": \"`in_memory_layer` was inserted between `decode`'s doc comment and `decode`. `decode` loses its doc, and the helper's rustdoc starts with \\\"An answer parsed from its text straight into `T`, in one pass.\\\"\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The generated docs describe `in_memory_layer` with two unrelated sentences and `decode` has none. A `missing_docs`/rustdoc lint in `just doc-check` may flag it, and readers are misled.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 568,\n    \"summary\": \"`type_memo` is now computed on every run, before the tracing macro. Before, it sat inside `tracing::debug!` and only ran when debug logging was enabled.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Every production run iterates all converters and calls `memo_stats`, which may lock each memo, even with debug tracing off. It is wasted work on the hot path. Compute it lazily, or only `if tracing::enabled!(Level::DEBUG)`.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 369,\n    \"summary\": \"The change adds interior mutability (`others: Mutex&lt;Vec&lt;u64&gt;&gt;`) to every Rpc, plus a second release entry point, to special-case the lanes' early release. Ownership could express this instead.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Every Rpc constructor (7 sites) now carries `Mutex::default()`, and the release-once invariant is enforced at runtime by `take_lanes`. A simpler shape: have `open_programs` return the first program and the lane programs separately, and let `session` release the lanes it owns when they finish. Then `release` needs no lane list and no Mutex.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"docs/type-checker-integration.md\",\n    \"line\": 202,\n    \"summary\": \"The Cost bullet has a broken sentence (\\\"It costs, three requests for the file...\\\") and memory numbers that disagree with the changeset.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The docs say \\\"Chakra 1,881 to about 1,700 MiB\\\", while `.changeset/instantiation-without-second-program.md` says \\\"1,886 to 1,690 MiB\\\". Readers get two different measurements for the same change, and the stray comma makes the sentence ungrammatical.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 1074,\n    \"summary\": \"The test's `releases == Some(3)` assertion cannot detect a double release. The comment admits a failed release is counted, so a second release of the second lane's snapshot would push the count to 4. But the first lane's release is also counted whether or not it succeeds, so dropping one release while adding a duplicate still sums to 3.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Suppose a regression makes `release` re-release a lane (for example, `take_lanes` stops clearing) and also skips the layered snapshot's release. The count stays at 3 and the test passes. It should assert the exact set of released snapshot ids from the recording.\"\n  }\n]\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>78043</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>7</tool_uses><duration_ms>88480</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:58:22.623Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a94531663184643a3</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01SbNk8Vsrt33sgN4MFchpqb</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper/ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba/tasks/a94531663184643a3.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium 1335c3d2..46d28434\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 574,\n    \"summary\": \"In a Teardown::OneShot run, `drop(converters.take())` now drops the lanes' converters and their memos synchronously whenever any generic component exists, and `release_lanes().await` waits on release round-trips. OneShot exists to skip exactly this teardown work.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`caliper-docgen extract` on Chakra, which has generic components: the converters are dropped here, so the later `std::mem::forget(converters)` forgets `None`. The 35-50 ms teardown saving from the OneShot changelog comes back on every project with a generic component. Fix: when `request.teardown == OneShot`, `mem::forget` the taken converters instead of dropping them, or decide this on purpose and say so.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 557,\n    \"summary\": \"`layered` now sends the probe file through `updateSnapshot` changes (`fileSystem` together with `reconfigurePrograms`) instead of `createSnapshot`. The docs only establish that the `fileSystem` layer is read on `createSnapshot`, and the new unit test only exercises tsrs.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"If the Go `7.1.0-beta` checker, or a tsrs version, ignores `fileSystem` inside `updateSnapshot` changes, the reconfigured program has a root file that does not exist. `source_file_symbol(probe)` returns None, and every generic component quietly falls back to its structural props (\\\"the checker did not load the file...\\\"). The output regresses with no error.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 571,\n    \"summary\": \"The project id is taken as `response.projects.into_iter().next()`, the first project listed. It is not matched against `self.project`, and nothing checks that the reconfigure actually produced the program rooted at the probe file.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"In the on-demand path, `layered` derives from the kept watch snapshot. If the update response lists another project before the reconfigured one (the comment in `beside` says responses list programs made or changed), the returned Rpc's `project` points at the wrong program. Probe lookups then fail and all generic components fall back, or they read from the wrong program.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 575,\n    \"summary\": \"`release_lanes` permanently releases `programs[1..]`'s snapshots, but those `Arc&lt;Rpc&gt;` handles are still alive in `answer_opened` and still carry their now-dead snapshot ids. Nothing marks them as released.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Any later code that asks a lane after `answer_on`, such as a future retry or a reuse of `programs` by an early start, sends requests against released snapshots and gets Rejected errors. The only protection is the doc comment \\\"Nothing may ask a lane after\\\". Lanes should be consumed or the snapshot ids cleared so misuse is impossible.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 904,\n    \"summary\": \"`in_memory_layer` was inserted between `decode`'s doc comment and `decode`. `decode` loses its doc, and the helper's rustdoc starts with \\\"An answer parsed from its text straight into `T`, in one pass.\\\"\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The generated docs describe `in_memory_layer` with two unrelated sentences and `decode` has none. A `missing_docs`/rustdoc lint in `just doc-check` may flag it, and readers are misled.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 568,\n    \"summary\": \"`type_memo` is now computed on every run, before the tracing macro. Before, it sat inside `tracing::debug!` and only ran when debug logging was enabled.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Every production run iterates all converters and calls `memo_stats`, which may lock each memo, even with debug tracing off. It is wasted work on the hot path. Compute it lazily, or only `if tracing::enabled!(Level::DEBUG)`.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 369,\n    \"summary\": \"The change adds interior mutability (`others: Mutex&lt;Vec&lt;u64&gt;&gt;`) to every Rpc, plus a second release entry point, to special-case the lanes' early release. Ownership could express this instead.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Every Rpc constructor (7 sites) now carries `Mutex::default()`, and the release-once invariant is enforced at runtime by `take_lanes`. A simpler shape: have `open_programs` return the first program and the lane programs separately, and let `session` release the lanes it owns when they finish. Then `release` needs no lane list and no Mutex.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"docs/type-checker-integration.md\",\n    \"line\": 202,\n    \"summary\": \"The Cost bullet has a broken sentence (\\\"It costs, three requests for the file...\\\") and memory numbers that disagree with the changeset.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The docs say \\\"Chakra 1,881 to about 1,700 MiB\\\", while `.changeset/instantiation-without-second-program.md` says \\\"1,886 to 1,690 MiB\\\". Readers get two different measurements for the same change, and the stray comma makes the sentence ungrammatical.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/rpc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 1074,\n    \"summary\": \"The test's `releases == Some(3)` assertion cannot detect a double release. The comment admits a failed release is counted, so a second release of the second lane's snapshot would push the count to 4. But the first lane's release is also counted whether or not it succeeds, so dropping one release while adding a duplicate still sums to 3.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Suppose a regression makes `release` re-release a lane (for example, `take_lanes` stops clearing) and also skips the layered snapshot's release. The count stays at 3 and the test passes. It should assert the exact set of released snapshot ids from the recording.\"\n  }\n]\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>78043</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>7</tool_uses><duration_ms>88480</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","reason":"delivered_to_agent"}
