{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-16T19:30:04.290Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a06913053f3c9633f</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01EG5uUrwWT7UvKD7YTo32c8</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper/ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba/tasks/a06913053f3c9633f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium 24438c48..2f234371\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 270,\n    \"summary\": \"With `unknown`/`is_complete` gone, a wrapper that spreads its props onto a tag that did not resolve (for example a third-party component) now hides every member of a large non-ambient dependency source that it does not name. The old code left those members to the resolver's collapse rule.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`function Select(p: ReactSelectProps) { return &lt;RS {...p} /&gt; }`, where `RS` is imported from node_modules, so `forwarding` gives `Target::Unresolved`. `ReactSelectProps` has about 70 members, so the resolver lists them all (non-ambient dependency, at most LARGE_API_SOURCE=200). Before this change they were `unknown` and stayed listed. Now `large` is true, `used` is false and they are not `declared`, so all of them go to `collapsed` and `narrow` moves them into a layer. The ADR says this case 'hides nothing', but the wrapper's whole forwarded API disappears from Controls.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 259,\n    \"summary\": \"`large` hard-codes COLLAPSE_THRESHOLD (30) for every source. `CollapseInheritedProps::collapses` uses LARGE_API_SOURCE (200) for non-ambient dependency sources, so there are now two disagreeing owners of 'is this source an API or a generated set'.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A dependency's own component props interface with 31 to 200 members (Radix or MUI component props, react-select `Props`). The resolver keeps it listed as 'the API a reader needs typed' (options.rs:83). Relevance then calls it large and moves every member the body does not name into a layer. The 'main props in full' contract fails for any dependency API over 30 members, and the cut-off disagrees with the resolver's documented rule. Fix: derive `large` from `collapses(...)` or share one threshold.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 249,\n    \"summary\": \"`reach` is keyed by the `(parent.name, parent.file_name)` / `(layer.type_name, layer.file_name)` strings. Different sources that share a key are summed together, and one source spelled two ways is split.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"(1) Several small inline type literals in one .d.ts all have parent `__type` with the same file, so their counts add up past 30. Each small literal source is then marked large and its members collapse. (2) A layer named after an alias (`type StyleProps = SystemProps`) next to listed rows whose `parent` is the underlying interface splits the count across two keys. A source of more than 30 members can then count as small, and its whole generated set is listed.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 515,\n    \"summary\": \"A listed prop with no `parent` is never large, so members of a type-literal or mapped source the answer lists without a parent are always API rows, however large the source.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The structural answer lists the props of a large type-literal style source (more than 30 members, declared in a type literal, so no `parent`). The doc comment says these props have 'no known source and [are] not large'. `api` is then true for every one of them and all of them become rows. Before this change, a non-ambient member with no evidence followed the collapse rule.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/extractor/body_facts.rs\",\n    \"line\": 316,\n    \"summary\": \"`BodyFacts::handed_on` is still computed at about 10 sites, stored in `types/collected.rs` and serialized into the cache, but nothing reads it any more since `Forwarding::handed_on` and `Body::handed_on` were removed.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Dead state that is computed, cached and tested (body_facts/tests.rs asserts it about 15 times). The module docs at body_facts.rs:25-30 still say `reads` is complete only while `handed_on` is false, which misleads the next reader about a contract nothing enforces. Remove the field (bumping CACHE_SCHEMA_VERSION) or note that it is unused.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 43,\n    \"summary\": \"The new comment says the resolver collapses a style system 'past `LARGE_API_SOURCE` members', but this code path's notion of large is COLLAPSE_THRESHOLD, and an ambient dependency style system collapses past COLLAPSE_THRESHOLD.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A reader trusts the comment and assumes the cut-off is 200. In fact relevance treats anything over 30 as large and the resolver collapses ambient dependency style sources over 30. The threshold mismatch in the second finding is hidden by this wrong comment.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 5,\n    \"summary\": \"The module docs still describe four outcomes ('a typed row, a name in its collapsed layer's `used` list, left in that layer, or unknown because the body says nothing'), but `Shown` now has only `rows` and `collapsed`.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The documented contract of `decide` no longer matches its output. A maintainer looking for the 'unknown' path, or for ambient-read members going to `used`, finds neither.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/forwarding/tests.rs\",\n    \"line\": 97,\n    \"summary\": \"The test helper `inherited()` was changed to `large: true`, so the unresolved-spread and handed-on tests (still named `..._is_unknown`) now cover only large sources. No test covers a non-ambient dependency source of 31 to 200 members behind an unresolved spread, the case where the behaviour regressed.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`a_spread_onto_a_tag_that_does_not_resolve_is_unknown` and `a_component_whose_props_did_not_resolve_is_unknown` now assert `collapsed`, so the regression in the first finding is locked in as expected behaviour, and the test names contradict their assertions.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 272,\n    \"summary\": \"An ambient member the signature defaults (`{ className = 'x' }`), or that the body only reads, is now a full typed row in `props` instead of a `used` name in the DOM layer.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Every component that destructures `className`, `children`, `style` or `onClick` with or without a default now gets typed Controls rows for those DOM attributes, as the snapshot growth shows. Under the old ADR Combination rule these stayed layer names. If the decision intends only body-'used' members, this increases the rows for most shadcn-style components.\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nI accidentally ran `git checkout 2f234371` in this worktree, which detached HEAD. I switched back to `fix/list-main-props` right away; it is the same commit. The worktree has an uncommitted change to `crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs` that I did not make and did not touch. It was outside the review range, so I did not review it.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>84081</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>9</tool_uses><duration_ms>115876</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
