{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-16T21:54:52.592Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ae796580b55e11392</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01VJ5oKuMcEpzZoAQjKWH5sE</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper/ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba/tasks/ae796580b55e11392.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium d884334e..4c22b134\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I found 10 issues in `d884334e..4c22b134`, most severe first. The top one is a real disagreement between the CLI and the Storybook session. I only read the code: I ran no builds or tests, and as instructed I did not run verifiers on these findings.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/types/semantic.rs\",\n    \"line\": 434,\n    \"summary\": \"The CLI's whole-project checker merge (`apply_props`, and its request at crates/semantic/src/lib.rs:106 with `react_wanted: Some(entry.props.keys())`) still takes React props only through `react_prop_listed`. Only the session merge applies the new `react_attribute_mode`, so under `collapseInheritedProps: none` with `htmlAttributes: full` the two lanes disagree.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Take a component with props `ReturnType&lt;typeof makeProps&gt;` returning `HTMLAttributes &amp; {label}`, run with collapse `none` and `full`. The Storybook/WatchSession lane lists children/className/id/label/ref, but `caliper extract` lists only `label`. That is the lane split ADR 0028 was written to remove, and the changeset says the CLI lists these too. The new test (`the_expanded_view_keeps_the_react_props_only_the_checker_finds`) covers only the session lane.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/resolver/relevance_inputs.rs\",\n    \"line\": 55,\n    \"summary\": \"`private_sources` checks `marked_private` on every non-React source, including the project's own interfaces. This change also starts narrowing project sources, so a project interface with an `@internal` or `@private` JSDoc now has its members treated as ambient and hidden.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Say the project has `/** @internal */ export interface ButtonBaseProps { loading?: boolean; spinner?: ReactNode }` and `ButtonProps extends ButtonBaseProps`. `PropsType::bases` skips the base, `members` marks `loading`/`spinner` ambient, `decide` drops them unless the body reads them, and `narrow` (no longer guarded for Project) moves them into a layer as names. Before this change they were listed rows.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/extractor/jsdoc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 48,\n    \"summary\": \"The gap check now chains backward through every comment, including already-consumed doc comments of sibling members. Each lookup for an undocumented declaration can therefore walk all earlier closely spaced comments, which is quadratic per file.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"In a large .d.ts where documented and undocumented members alternate, each with lines under 120 bytes (common in @types/react, lib.dom-style typings and generated style-prop files), every undocumented member walks back over all prior consumed comments, about n²/4 iterations. Before, the walk stopped 120 bytes from the declaration.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/extractor/jsdoc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 43,\n    \"summary\": \"With the chained gap, a doc comment any distance up a run of `//` or `/* */` lines (for example a file header) now attaches to the first declaration after the run. Its tags, including `@private` or `@internal`, then make that declaration ambient.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A file starts with `/** @packageDocumentation @internal */`, then several `// eslint-disable…`/license lines, then `export interface CardProps {…}`. Before, the header was more than 120 bytes away and ignored. Now `CardProps` gets the header's description and `internal` tag, `marked_private` is true, and its members are hidden as names.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 168,\n    \"summary\": \"`home()` still picks the element layer with the path-based `declaration_origin(..).is_ambient()`. `SharedDom` is no longer ambient by path, so a left-out member of React Aria's private `GlobalDOMAttributes`/`GlobalDOMEvents` no longer joins the HTML element layer.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A React Aria component lists `dir` (parent `GlobalDOMAttributes` in `@react-types/shared/src/dom.d.ts`, `@private`), and the decision leaves it out. Before, it joined the element layer. Now `home` makes a new `GlobalDOMAttributes` layer, so output layers change and the two ambient sources (React and the private ones) are no longer grouped the same way. The per-declaration `private` set is not consulted here.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/types/ambient.rs\",\n    \"line\": 96,\n    \"summary\": \"`is_dom_file` keeps the whole `@react-types/shared/src/dom.d.ts` file on the 30-member ambient collapse threshold. It relies on a comment claiming only `GlobalDOMEvents` there exceeds 30 members, so per-declaration ambient is only partly applied (an altitude issue).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Suppose a documented API interface in that file reaches more than 30 members through its bases, for example a text-input DOM props interface extending several others. It collapses into a layer, so it is `large`, and it is hidden as names. That contradicts ADR 0028's rule that API is listed up to 200 members, with no diagnostic.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 248,\n    \"summary\": \"The new `ambient` closure allocates two `String`s (`type_name.to_owned()`, `file.to_owned()`) on every call. It runs once per member inside the per-layer loop, even though the layer's answer is the same for all its members.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A Chakra component with a `SystemProperties` layer of thousands of members makes thousands of String pairs plus a path scan per component on each `apply`. Compute `ambient` once per layer before the member loop, and key `private` so a borrowed `(&amp;str, &amp;str)` can look it up.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 146,\n    \"summary\": \"`narrow` now clones every layer member name into a `BTreeSet&lt;String&gt;` on every call, including when no prop is left out. Before, it allocated nothing in that case.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`narrow` runs on `apply` and again at the end of every checker merge (session and CLI). A component with a 4,000-member layer pays about 4,000 String allocations and a tree build per call. Build the set lazily on the first left-out prop, or use borrowed keys or indices.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/react_types.rs\",\n    \"line\": 455,\n    \"summary\": \"The doc comment on `react_prop_listed` still reads \\\"[`checker_prop_used`] without the mode it no longer reads\\\". This change makes `checker_prop_used` read `mode` again (`Full` takes every React prop).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A maintainer reading this doc will assume mode is ignored and keep both merges on `react_prop_listed`, which is how the CLI/session split in finding 1 happened.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 137,\n    \"summary\": \"This breaks /Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-abdd35c983e9e5db8/AGENTS.md: \\\"Comments: only the non-obvious why, no history (the commit records it). Fill to 120 columns\\\". The rewritten doc line is 139 columns, and line 90 adds history: \\\"(Entire finding on change 288)\\\".\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Other added lines also exceed 120 columns: origin.rs module doc (121), `is_ambient_declaration` doc (123), props_type.rs comment at about line 264 (122), ambient.rs doc (121). rustfmt does not re-fill comments, so `just lint` will not catch them. The history reference in non-test code goes stale.\"\n  }\n]\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>116818</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>22</tool_uses><duration_ms>250214</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
