{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-17T01:37:46.544Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac0a58018547242af</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01TTJRKSfSv4ohJG5TucYpeo</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper/ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba/tasks/ac0a58018547242af.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium 1a6fc135..78aec45d\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/convert.rs\",\n    \"line\": 518,\n    \"summary\": \"Whether 0 and -0 stay apart now depends on `fingerprint`, not on equality. `PropType`'s derived `PartialEq` says `NumberLiteral(0.0) == NumberLiteral(-0.0)`, and only `to_bits()` in `fingerprint` puts them in different buckets.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Suppose a later change adds an f64 to some variant (in `LiteralUnion`, `ObjectField` or `TupleElement`, say) and `fingerprint` skips it, as it already skips flags and labels. A tree with `-0` then lands in the bucket of an equal-by-`==` tree with `0`. `share` hands back the `0` tree, and the memo recalls `0` where `-0` was converted, so the output changes for every later hit. The doc comment says leaving fields out 'only puts more types in one bucket… which tells them apart by equality'. That is false for float sign, so the invariant is undocumented and fragile.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/convert.rs\",\n    \"line\": 519,\n    \"summary\": \"`share` computes the full-tree `fingerprint` and runs the deep-equality scan while holding one mutex that every lane of the session shares.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Every memo miss on every lane takes the same `Trees` lock. That includes trivial ones such as `PropType::String`. Under the lock it walks the whole converted tree (up to `MAX_SIZE` nodes) and deep-compares it against each bucket member. Lanes that used to convert in parallel now serialize on this lock. Computing `fingerprint(converted)` before taking the lock would remove most of the time spent holding it. Sharding the map, or skipping leaf types, would remove the contention.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/convert.rs\",\n    \"line\": 926,\n    \"summary\": \"A memo hit still deep-copies the shared tree (`PropType::clone(&amp;entry.converted)`), so only the memo's own copies are deduplicated. The trees returned to callers are not.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Chakra's 3.6M memo hits each still build a full copy of the style-prop tree. The comment above even notes that hits 'can build gigabytes of them between two requests'. Peak memory from hit copies stays as it was. A deeper fix would let `convert` return `Arc&lt;PropType&gt;`, or share subtrees, so a recall costs one refcount bump.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/convert.rs\",\n    \"line\": 521,\n    \"summary\": \"A tree that holds NaN never equals anything, so each share of it adds another entry to the same bucket, and each later lookup deep-compares against all of them.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Take a type containing `NumberLiteral(NAN)` or `EnumMember` with a NaN value, reached N times on misses (at different depths or lanes). The bucket grows to N entries, and each insert scans all of them with a deep `==`. That is O(N²) comparisons under the global lock, and the bucket keeps every copy, so nothing is deduplicated. The test enshrines this behaviour rather than bounding it.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/convert.rs\",\n    \"line\": 510,\n    \"summary\": \"`Trees` keeps every distinct tree for the whole session and has no way to evict, so a tree stays held even when only one memo entry ever referenced it.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"On a project like shadcn, where few props share types (the changeset says it does not change), each converted tree now pays for a `Vec` slot, an `Arc` header and a hashmap entry on top of what it cost before. The `Arc` also outlives any future memo pruning, because `by_fingerprint` holds its own reference. The result is a small memory regression on low-sharing projects, and any later memo eviction will not free these trees.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/convert.rs\",\n    \"line\": 941,\n    \"summary\": \"On a miss whose tree is new, `share(&amp;converted)` deep-clones the tree into the `Arc` while the original is returned, so a first conversion costs two full trees.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"For a large new tree (up to `MAX_SIZE`) the clone doubles that conversion's transient allocation. It also runs after `crate::heap::step()`, so the heap budget can be crossed without being checked. Moving `converted` into the `Arc` and returning a clone only when a caller needs it, or reusing the owned value, would avoid the extra copy.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 506,\n    \"summary\": \"Only the session's lanes share `Trees`. `instantiate::answer` builds its own `Converter::new`, which gets a fresh `State::default()` and therefore its own empty `Trees`, even though it converts the same library's style props.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Generic components on Chakra go through the instantiate path, which rebuilds and keeps its own copy of every distinct style tree. Peak memory during that phase does not benefit from the sharing. The lanes' `Trees` is dropped just before (lib.rs:574), so passing it on is not a fix either. If the instantiate phase is the peak, the measured -19% may not hold there.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/convert.rs\",\n    \"line\": 436,\n    \"summary\": \"The doc comment on `push_fields` still says it is 'for [`past_size_budget`]', but it is now also called through `push_inner` from `fingerprint`.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Someone changing `push_fields` for `past_size_budget`'s needs, such as skipping index keys, would also change bucketing in `fingerprint` without noticing. A stale comment hides a shared dependency.\"\n  }\n]\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>74839</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>7</tool_uses><duration_ms>73268</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
