{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-17T01:51:51.389Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a291ddfa8c47e3e3f</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_015SphAYGF8ZpjPvbQbYFJ1s</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper/ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba/tasks/a291ddfa8c47e3e3f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium 4c22b134..56192ec7\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/extractor/jsdoc.rs\",\n    \"line\": 49,\n    \"summary\": \"`next_start` now moves only for `//` comments, so a plain `/* */` note between a doc comment and its declaration counts toward the 120-byte proximity gap, and a long note breaks the walk before the doc comment is reached.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`/** Button docs */\\\\n/* a 150-byte eslint/explanatory block note */\\\\nexport interface ButtonProps {}`: before the change the gap was measured to the note's start and the doc was found. Now `span_start - doc.span_end` &gt; 120, the loop breaks, and ButtonProps (or a prop) silently loses its description and tags. This contradicts the comment two lines below, which says a plain `/* */` note is skipped so the doc comment before it still counts.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/resolver/alias.rs\",\n    \"line\": 565,\n    \"summary\": \"`copies_a_generated_key_set` returns false for the whole key set when any key lacks a `parent`. Before, each key with a parent past the threshold was flagged on its own.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`{ [K in keyof Conditions | 'extra']?: V }` or `keyof Conditions | SomeLiteral`: the single literal key makes `any(parent.is_none())` true, so none of Conditions' 130 copied keys is marked `copied_from_large_keys`. All 130 become typed rows instead of names, which is a regression from the old per-key rule.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/pipeline/semantic_hook.rs\",\n    \"line\": 221,\n    \"summary\": \"The mode now comes from `expanded(&amp;answer.entry)`, which is false whenever `entry.relevance` is None. So under `collapseInheritedProps: none` an answer without a placement falls back to Curated instead of the run's `full` mode.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Gathering relevance evidence panics: `contain_panic` gives None, `decide` skips `apply`, and `relevance` stays None. In collapse:none the session merge then drops every React prop the checker found that the structural pass did not list (the `ReturnType&lt;typeof makeProps&gt;` case this change set out to fix). The refine cache key hashes only Layering, not the mode, so an answer cached under one mode can also be reused under the other.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 106,\n    \"summary\": \"`targets_with` decides expanded from `cx.mode`, but the merge (`types/semantic.rs` `apply_props`) decides it from `entry.relevance` (`Placement::expanded`). That gives two owners for the same decision, against the ADR's 'One owner' rule, and they can disagree.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`targets_of(output)` (cx None, used by tests and kept.rs) asks only for the listed React props, while the merge of a collapse:none output treats the entry as expanded and expects every React prop. The reverse also happens when relevance is None but cx.mode is None. Either way the request and the merge disagree: React props are asked for but dropped, or expected but never converted.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/resolver/chain.rs\",\n    \"line\": 598,\n    \"summary\": \"`state.body_reads` runs for every member of every collapsed source and records one `(name, bool)` entry per member in the memo reads. Each memo hit then re-checks all of them, each with a linear scan of `body.reads` (memo.rs:162).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Chakra's `SystemProperties`/`Conditions` (hundreds to thousands of members): each recording carries thousands of body_reads entries, and every lookup costs O(members × body.reads). Components with different read sets can no longer share a recording, so recordings multiply per distinct body. Peak RSS and time rise on Chakra+deps. A cheaper option is to record only `body.reads ∩ members`, or to check that intersection once per source.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/resolver/alias.rs\",\n    \"line\": 573,\n    \"summary\": \"The table check formats each key's `CollectedType` with `Debug` into a new String every time a mapped chain over more than 30 keys resolves. It also runs when `keys.len() &gt; LARGE_API_SOURCE || react` has already decided the answer.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Chakra-style mapped types over 130 to 1000+ keys allocate and format a (possibly nested) type string per key on every resolution, and the first key's string is built twice, all in the hot resolver path. Checking `large || react` first and comparing structurally (or deriving `PartialEq`) avoids that work.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/resolver/alias.rs\",\n    \"line\": 573,\n    \"summary\": \"The 'table' heuristic marks any interface of more than 30 members that are all required and share one declared type as generated, whatever it means.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"A project `Labels` interface of 35 required `string` members (i18n labels) or a `Flags` interface of 35 required `boolean` members, copied as `{ [K in keyof Labels]?: string }` into a component's props: all 35 real API props are demoted to layer names instead of typed Controls rows.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/tests/relevance_model.rs\",\n    \"line\": 381,\n    \"summary\": \"A new test comment records history, against CLAUDE.md's 'Comments: only the non-obvious why, no history (the commit records it).' (AGENTS.md, Writing and commits). The same diff removed an identical '(Entire finding on change 288)' note from relevance/mod.rs for that reason.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"`// Entire finding on change 288 (react_types.rs:452), the CLI's whole-project merge: ...` embeds a finding ID and a line number that will go stale. This breaks the AGENTS.md comment rule.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/tests/relevance_model.rs\",\n    \"line\": 274,\n    \"summary\": \"The test helper `both_lanes` calls `std::os::unix::fs::symlink` with no `#[cfg(unix)]` gate, and it needs `node_modules/@types/react` from a pnpm install in the workspace.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"On a Windows target the test crate fails to compile. On a CI job without `pnpm install`, every `both_lanes` test (including the existing per-declaration test) panics on `canonicalize(...).expect`.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"docs/adr/0028-relevance-evidence-and-the-checker-scheduler.md\",\n    \"line\": 56,\n    \"summary\": \"The amended ADR text repeats a phrase: 'a size-only rule at 30 a size-only rule at 30 would catch...'.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"The decision record has a garbled sentence in its rationale paragraph. Readers have to guess what was meant, and the typos/doc review won't catch it.\"\n  }\n]\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>115300</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>19</tool_uses><duration_ms>185309</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
