{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-17T02:48:13.403Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a54ae5b13d05427f1</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01BHy4vTbczJeZG5JXAD6fcS</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-agent-abdd35c983e9e5db8/ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba/tasks/a54ae5b13d05427f1.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium cf51133b..a589e64a\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I found 9 findings in `cf51133b..a589e64a`, ranked most severe first. As asked, they are not verified, and I ran no builds or tests. Line numbers refer to the files at `a589e64a`.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/react_types.rs\",\n    \"line\": 455,\n    \"summary\": \"visible_rows returns only entry.props keys, but both checker merges filter with it before layer_checker_props runs. So a @types/react member the structural answer keeps typed in notableInherited (className, style, or a member the body reads from a React layer) is dropped by checker_prop_kept and is never asked for in react_wanted.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Props are `Huge &amp; HTMLAttributes&lt;HTMLDivElement&gt;` and the body reads `props.title`. The structural answer keeps `title` in notable_inherited, not props. In both semantic_hook::apply and semantic::apply_props, checker_prop_kept(Curated, \\\"title\\\", react_declared=true, visible) returns false, and react_props_wanted_for leaves `title` out of the request. layer_notable now lists `title` (the change made for this case), but no checker prop reaches it, so `title` keeps its structural type in the checker lane. That contradicts the commit's claim that it is 'visible to every owner' and the doc on visible_rows.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 100,\n    \"summary\": \"The new top-level `Omit` alias arm reads its base with `read.part(base)`. That inherits every member of the base interface, including the omitted keys, into `inherited`. An `Omit` written inline (part → pick + inherit(\\\"Omit\\\")) inherits nothing.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"With `type Props = Omit&lt;BaseProps, 'onChange'&gt;; function C(props: Props)`, reachable_members() now includes `onChange` and the other BaseProps members. Forwarding treats `onChange` as reaching the target even though the type removes it. Writing `props: Omit&lt;BaseProps,'onChange'&gt;` inline gives a different answer from the alias form.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 128,\n    \"summary\": \"A single unproven `Pick` anywhere in the props type sets unproven_pick for the whole component. This disables narrowing and switches both checker merges to the expanded `Full` path for all @types/react props, including those from an unrelated intersected `HTMLAttributes`.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"In `Pick&lt;OwnProps, keyof Chosen&gt; &amp; React.HTMLAttributes&lt;HTMLDivElement&gt;`, the unproven key set only concerns OwnProps. Still, Placement::expanded() becomes true, react_wanted becomes None, and checker_prop_kept takes every React attribute the checker reports (about 250 DOM props). Relevance no longer narrows any of them, so the default view becomes the expanded listing of all DOM attributes. The flag should be scoped to the Pick's own source.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 179,\n    \"summary\": \"The `Omit` and `Partial|Required|Readonly` arms of `picks` match only a `CollectedType::Named` base. Generic-alias instantiation now produces `AtFile`-wrapped arguments, and these arms do not unwrap them with written_at, so the substituted Pick's keys are lost.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"With `type Ro&lt;T&gt; = Readonly&lt;T&gt;;` and `props: Ro&lt;Pick&lt;Attrs,'id'|'title'&gt;&gt;`, the instantiated Passthrough target is `Readonly&lt;AtFile{Pick&lt;…&gt;}&gt;`. picks(\\\"Readonly\\\", [AtFile]) misses its arm and falls to `(_, args)`, where lookup(\\\"Readonly\\\") returns None. No key is picked and relevance may narrow the selected `id` and `title` out of the listing.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/examples/gate_harness.rs\",\n    \"line\": 363,\n    \"summary\": \"gate_harness still builds react_wanted with the old react_props_wanted and decides expanded from the run's option alone. The session now decides from the placement (react_types::expanded) and react_props_wanted_for.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"For a component whose placement is expanded because of an unproven Pick, the session asks the checker for all React props (None), while the harness asks for Some(props keys). The comment 'the same request the session sends' is now false, and the gate's query timings measure a smaller request than production sends.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/react_types.rs\",\n    \"line\": 425,\n    \"summary\": \"checker_prop_used and react_prop_listed have no callers left, and react_props_wanted's only caller is the gate_harness example. They duplicate the new checker_prop_kept and react_props_wanted_for, and stale doc comments still point at them (semantic.rs:182, semantic.rs:429).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"AGENTS.md says to 'delete what a change makes dead in the same PR'. These stay `pub`, so no dead-code lint fires, and two parallel filters now exist that can drift apart. gate_harness already uses the old one.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 484,\n    \"summary\": \"targets_of (cx None) silently treats a missing CollapseContext as `Dependencies`, so an entry with no placement always gets a restricted react_wanted, whatever collapse mode the merge will actually use.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Suppose a caller produces the structural output under `collapseInheritedProps: none` with no relevance placement, builds targets with targets_of, and merges with cx.mode None. The request lists only the props keys while apply_props takes every React prop (Full), so React props the structural pass missed (`ReturnType&lt;typeof makeProps&gt;`) are never reported.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 62,\n    \"summary\": \"`inputs.narrows &amp;&amp; !inputs.props_type.unproven_pick` is computed twice, once for `left` and once for `narrows`, and the `left` guard adds nothing because narrow() is a no-op when narrows is false.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Duplicated derived state: compute `let narrows = …` once and use it for both. As written, a future edit to one condition can leave Placement.left and Placement.narrows disagreeing.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/resolver/relevance_inputs.rs\",\n    \"line\": 410,\n    \"summary\": \"Every lookup with type arguments clones the whole alias and runs apply_generic_args, and the props type name is looked up twice: read.pick(name, args) and then lookup(name, args) at the top of read. part() also repeats resolve_to_canonical through pick and then inherit.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"For each component whose props type is a generic alias such as `Select&lt;Attrs,'id'&gt;`, the alias is cloned and instantiated at least twice per resolution, and again for each intersected part. This is wasted work on a per-component path. Return Placement-free key data from one lookup, or skip the instantiation when the caller only needs keys of a non-generic alias.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/types/semantic.rs\",\n    \"line\": 434,\n    \"summary\": \"visible_rows clones every prop name into a new BTreeSet&lt;String&gt; on each merge (apply_props per component in the whole-project merge, and semantic_hook::apply), only to call contains(). Before, the code checked entry.props directly.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"One String allocation per prop per component on the merge path, for large component libraries (Chakra or Mantine with hundreds of props), with no behaviour gained. Taking a `&amp;BTreeMap` or a key-membership closure gives the same answer without allocating.\"\n  }\n]\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>107352</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>15</tool_uses><duration_ms>144668</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-11-17T02:48:13.481Z","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a54ae5b13d05427f1</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01BHy4vTbczJeZG5JXAD6fcS</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-caliper--claude-worktrees-agent-abdd35c983e9e5db8/ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba/tasks/a54ae5b13d05427f1.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"/code-review medium cf51133b..a589e64a\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I found 9 findings in `cf51133b..a589e64a`, ranked most severe first. As asked, they are not verified, and I ran no builds or tests. Line numbers refer to the files at `a589e64a`.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/react_types.rs\",\n    \"line\": 455,\n    \"summary\": \"visible_rows returns only entry.props keys, but both checker merges filter with it before layer_checker_props runs. So a @types/react member the structural answer keeps typed in notableInherited (className, style, or a member the body reads from a React layer) is dropped by checker_prop_kept and is never asked for in react_wanted.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Props are `Huge &amp; HTMLAttributes&lt;HTMLDivElement&gt;` and the body reads `props.title`. The structural answer keeps `title` in notable_inherited, not props. In both semantic_hook::apply and semantic::apply_props, checker_prop_kept(Curated, \\\"title\\\", react_declared=true, visible) returns false, and react_props_wanted_for leaves `title` out of the request. layer_notable now lists `title` (the change made for this case), but no checker prop reaches it, so `title` keeps its structural type in the checker lane. That contradicts the commit's claim that it is 'visible to every owner' and the doc on visible_rows.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 100,\n    \"summary\": \"The new top-level `Omit` alias arm reads its base with `read.part(base)`. That inherits every member of the base interface, including the omitted keys, into `inherited`. An `Omit` written inline (part → pick + inherit(\\\"Omit\\\")) inherits nothing.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"With `type Props = Omit&lt;BaseProps, 'onChange'&gt;; function C(props: Props)`, reachable_members() now includes `onChange` and the other BaseProps members. Forwarding treats `onChange` as reaching the target even though the type removes it. Writing `props: Omit&lt;BaseProps,'onChange'&gt;` inline gives a different answer from the alias form.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 128,\n    \"summary\": \"A single unproven `Pick` anywhere in the props type sets unproven_pick for the whole component. This disables narrowing and switches both checker merges to the expanded `Full` path for all @types/react props, including those from an unrelated intersected `HTMLAttributes`.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"In `Pick&lt;OwnProps, keyof Chosen&gt; &amp; React.HTMLAttributes&lt;HTMLDivElement&gt;`, the unproven key set only concerns OwnProps. Still, Placement::expanded() becomes true, react_wanted becomes None, and checker_prop_kept takes every React attribute the checker reports (about 250 DOM props). Relevance no longer narrows any of them, so the default view becomes the expanded listing of all DOM attributes. The flag should be scoped to the Pick's own source.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs\",\n    \"line\": 179,\n    \"summary\": \"The `Omit` and `Partial|Required|Readonly` arms of `picks` match only a `CollectedType::Named` base. Generic-alias instantiation now produces `AtFile`-wrapped arguments, and these arms do not unwrap them with written_at, so the substituted Pick's keys are lost.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"With `type Ro&lt;T&gt; = Readonly&lt;T&gt;;` and `props: Ro&lt;Pick&lt;Attrs,'id'|'title'&gt;&gt;`, the instantiated Passthrough target is `Readonly&lt;AtFile{Pick&lt;…&gt;}&gt;`. picks(\\\"Readonly\\\", [AtFile]) misses its arm and falls to `(_, args)`, where lookup(\\\"Readonly\\\") returns None. No key is picked and relevance may narrow the selected `id` and `title` out of the listing.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/examples/gate_harness.rs\",\n    \"line\": 363,\n    \"summary\": \"gate_harness still builds react_wanted with the old react_props_wanted and decides expanded from the run's option alone. The session now decides from the placement (react_types::expanded) and react_props_wanted_for.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"For a component whose placement is expanded because of an unproven Pick, the session asks the checker for all React props (None), while the harness asks for Some(props keys). The comment 'the same request the session sends' is now false, and the gate's query timings measure a smaller request than production sends.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/react_types.rs\",\n    \"line\": 425,\n    \"summary\": \"checker_prop_used and react_prop_listed have no callers left, and react_props_wanted's only caller is the gate_harness example. They duplicate the new checker_prop_kept and react_props_wanted_for, and stale doc comments still point at them (semantic.rs:182, semantic.rs:429).\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"AGENTS.md says to 'delete what a change makes dead in the same PR'. These stay `pub`, so no dead-code lint fires, and two parallel filters now exist that can drift apart. gate_harness already uses the old one.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/semantic/src/lib.rs\",\n    \"line\": 484,\n    \"summary\": \"targets_of (cx None) silently treats a missing CollapseContext as `Dependencies`, so an entry with no placement always gets a restricted react_wanted, whatever collapse mode the merge will actually use.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Suppose a caller produces the structural output under `collapseInheritedProps: none` with no relevance placement, builds targets with targets_of, and merges with cx.mode None. The request lists only the props keys while apply_props takes every React prop (Full), so React props the structural pass missed (`ReturnType&lt;typeof makeProps&gt;`) are never reported.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/relevance/mod.rs\",\n    \"line\": 62,\n    \"summary\": \"`inputs.narrows &amp;&amp; !inputs.props_type.unproven_pick` is computed twice, once for `left` and once for `narrows`, and the `left` guard adds nothing because narrow() is a no-op when narrows is false.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"Duplicated derived state: compute `let narrows = …` once and use it for both. As written, a future edit to one condition can leave Placement.left and Placement.narrows disagreeing.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/resolver/relevance_inputs.rs\",\n    \"line\": 410,\n    \"summary\": \"Every lookup with type arguments clones the whole alias and runs apply_generic_args, and the props type name is looked up twice: read.pick(name, args) and then lookup(name, args) at the top of read. part() also repeats resolve_to_canonical through pick and then inherit.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"For each component whose props type is a generic alias such as `Select&lt;Attrs,'id'&gt;`, the alias is cloned and instantiated at least twice per resolution, and again for each intersected part. This is wasted work on a per-component path. Return Placement-free key data from one lookup, or skip the instantiation when the caller only needs keys of a non-generic alias.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"file\": \"crates/core/src/types/semantic.rs\",\n    \"line\": 434,\n    \"summary\": \"visible_rows clones every prop name into a new BTreeSet&lt;String&gt; on each merge (apply_props per component in the whole-project merge, and semantic_hook::apply), only to call contains(). Before, the code checked entry.props directly.\",\n    \"failure_scenario\": \"One String allocation per prop per component on the merge path, for large component libraries (Chakra or Mantine with hundreds of props), with no behaviour gained. Taking a `&amp;BTreeMap` or a key-membership closure gives the same answer without allocating.\"\n  }\n]\n```</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>107352</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>15</tool_uses><duration_ms>144668</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","reason":"delivered_to_agent"}
